.TH CBMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_create_full \- create a BST map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cbstmap.h>

cbmap cbmap_create_full(ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_comparison_proc_t custom_comparison_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a self-balancing BST map with full control over the key type,
memory management, and comparison function.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
key_type                 Type of the keys stored in the map
mmgmt_procs              Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_comparison_proc   Custom comparison function, or NULL
err                      Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created BST map on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Default comparison for ccol_char: this platform's own native char comparison
(signed or unsigned, matching however char behaves on this platform)
Default comparison for ccol_signed_char (a scalar signed char or its
typedef int8_t; a signed char * string key is ccol_string, not this) and
short/int/long/long_long: signed comparison, independent of this
platform's own char signedness
Default comparison for the unsigned integer types and ccol_pointer: unsigned
comparison
Default comparison for ccol_float/double/long_double: numeric floating-point
comparison; a NaN key sorts as greater than every non-NaN key and equal to
every other NaN key
Default comparison for ccol_string: strcmp-equivalent lexicographic order
For ccol_other_types (e.g. a struct key type), the default falls back to a
raw memcmp of the representation, comparing indeterminate padding bytes and
any pointer members by address rather than by pointee; pass
custom_comparison_proc for a struct key
Custom comparison receives void* pointers to key data
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_create (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
